# Unattended Stata execution

Use this workflow for terminal, scheduled, or remote Stata runs. Preserve the
project's scientific assertions and package or plugin checks when changing how
Stata starts and completes.

## Choose and qualify the launcher

Invoke the Stata executable directly through the bundled
[`scripts/stata_runner.py`](../scripts/stata_runner.py) helper. It uses the
Python standard library and supports macOS and Linux process groups; Windows
fails explicitly and needs a separately qualified launcher.

For a macOS app-bundle binary, use unattended `-e` execution. The [Stata Mac manual, section B.3](https://www.stata.com/manuals/gsmb.pdf)
documents `-e do <file>` and automatic exit; `-b` can leave a completion dialog.
The helper uses `-e <wrapper>` because that spelling executed successfully on
the tested Stata/BE 18 installation, where `-e do <wrapper>` did not produce the
expected log. Qualify the actual executable and argument spelling after an
installation or version change. macOS app batch mode remains an application
process; it is not a guarantee of a true headless console environment.
For a genuine console executable, the helper uses `-b do <wrapper>` instead.

From the skill's root, an example run is:

```bash
python3 scripts/stata_runner.py \
  --stata "/Applications/Stata/Stata.app/Contents/MacOS/StataBE" \
  --do-file "/path/to/project/scripts/job.do" \
  --run-dir "/path/to/private/run outputs/new-run" \
  --cwd "/path/to/project" \
  --timeout 300
```

Select the actual executable and a new run directory; the helper retains the
wrapper, log, stdout, stderr, Stata return-code sidecar, and JSON result for
diagnosis. Keep these outside public source trees because Stata logs may contain
license or data metadata.
Set a timeout suitable for the workload.

## Verify completion

Success requires natural process exit and an exact run-specific Stata
return-code record in a fresh sidecar file; inspect the retained log separately.
An operating-system status of zero alone is insufficient. A completion marker
followed by forced termination is a failed unattended run. Timeout cleanup
targets only the process group created for that run; never use a broad Stata
process-name kill.

Before adopting the launcher, test a successful do-file, a deliberate Stata
error, and a timeout in fresh directories, including paths with spaces. Check
the retained evidence and absence of residual owned processes. A successful
launch does not establish numerical correctness or plugin fidelity.

Verify successful and deliberately failing runs while the screen is actually
locked before claiming locked-screen reliability. Desktop completion without
interaction does not establish that condition. Sleep, unavailable licenses,
and locked or unavailable data volumes remain separate execution constraints.

## Adopt in a project

Existing project launchers remain in place until deliberate migration. For a
reproducible pipeline, copy the reviewed helper into the project and record its
source commit or digest, then qualify that copy with the project's checks.
Avoid a runtime dependency on the mutable installed skill directory. Keep
rollback to the prior launcher available until acceptance succeeds.
